# Input, positions and errors

This file is the ground the rest of the library stands on: the text being parsed, how far into it
a parser has got, and what is reported when the text is not what the grammar wants. It is a
literate Avin file, so everything indented four columns is the program and everything at the margin
is this explanation, which the compiler never reads.

    module dev.avinlang.parsing

## Positions

A parser works in byte offsets, because that is what indexing a string costs nothing for. A person
reading an error wants a line and a column, and a column counted in characters: `é` is two bytes
but one column. A `Position` carries all three, so code that wants to slice the text and code that
wants to show the place to a person each have what they need.

    // A place in a text: the byte offset, and the line and column it falls on, both counted from 1. A
    // column counts characters, not bytes.
    data Position(offset: int, line: int, column: int)

A position shows as `line:column`, the form editors and compilers use, so an error message can be
clicked through to its place.

    impl Display for Position
        display(out: Writer, spec: FormatSpec) = out.write(s"$line:$column")

## Errors

When a parse fails, many alternatives have usually failed, at many places. The one worth reporting
is the **furthest**: every alternative that stopped earlier was wrong about the text sooner, and the
text up to the furthest failure is, by definition, something the grammar can read. So a
`ParseError` holds the furthest position reached, *everything* that could have continued the text
there, and what was found instead.

    // Why a parse failed: the furthest position any alternative reached, everything that could have
    // continued the text there, in the order the grammar tried them, and what was found instead (a
    // character in its quoted, escaped form, or `end of input`).
    data ParseError(position: Position, expected: Vector[string], found: string)

The expectations are kept in the order the grammar tried them, which is the order the grammar's
author wrote the alternatives in, so the message lists them in an order that person recognises.
They are joined the way a sentence joins them: `a`, `a or b`, `a, b or c`. An error that expected
nothing in particular, which happens when a look ahead refuses a character, says only what it
found.

    impl ParseError
        // The error as one line: `3:14: expected `)` or `,`, found 'x'`.
        message() -> string
            val out = StringWriter()
            out.write(s"${position.line}:${position.column}: ")
            val n = expected.length()
            if n == 0
                out.write("unexpected ")
            else
                out.write("expected ")
                for i in 0..<n
                    if i > 0 then out.write(if i == n - 1 then " or " else ", ")
                    out.write(expected(i))
                out.write(", found ")
            out.write(found)
            out.text()

    impl Display for ParseError
        display(out: Writer, spec: FormatSpec) = out.write(message())

## The input

An `Input` is the one mutable thing in a parse. Every parser reads from it and moves its offset;
none copies it. That choice is what makes the library cheap: a parser that succeeds allocates only
its value and the `Some` around it, and backtracking is restoring one integer. The alternative
design, an immutable input with every result carrying the input after it, allocates a result at
every step of every parse.

The input also remembers the furthest failure, and whether the parse has passed a **commit point**.
A commit point is where the grammar knows which rule it is in, so that a failure after it is final:
there is no point trying the next alternative from the start, and the error should name what the
committed rule wanted.

    // The text a parser reads and the state of reading it: the byte offset of the next character, the
    // furthest offset at which anything failed with what was expected there, and whether a failure has
    // passed a commit point. Parsers read and move `offset`; an alternative that fails is retried from
    // where it started.
    class Input(val text: string)
        // The text's length in bytes.
        val length = text.byteLength()
        // The byte offset of the next character to read.
        var offset = 0
        // Set when a parser fails after a commit point: no alternative is tried after it and the whole
        // parse fails.
        var committed = false

What has failed furthest, and what was expected there, are private: parsers record a failure with
`expect` and `fail` below and never read the record back. `known` is the last position the input
worked out, explained with `position`.

        private var furthest = -1
        private val expectations = Buffer[string]()
        // The last position worked out, from which the next one is counted when it lies further on.
        private var known = Position(0, 1, 1)

These two are what a hand-written parser needs to look at the text.

        // Whether every character has been read.
        atEnd() -> bool = offset >= length

        // The character at `offset`, which traps at the end of the text.
        current() -> char = text.charAt(offset)

### Recording what was expected

Every leaf parser that fails says what it wanted. Only the furthest offset's wishes are worth
keeping, since the text is right up to there: a failure further on replaces them, a failure at the
same place joins them, and a failure before it is forgotten.

        // Records that `what` was expected at `offset`. Only the furthest offset's expectations are kept,
        // since the text is right up to there.
        expect(what: string)
            if offset > furthest
                furthest = offset
                expectations.clear()
                expectations.add(what)
            elif offset == furthest
                expectations.add(what)

A parser fails by answering `None`, and almost always records why at the same moment, so `fail`
does both and reads as the last line of a parser that gives up: `else input.fail("integer")`.

        // Records that `what` was expected at `offset`, and fails.
        fail[T](what: string) -> Option[T]
            expect(what)
            None

### Labels

A rule made of many parts fails with the expectations of its parts: an expression that fails at its
first character expects an integer, `(`, `-`, and whatever else can begin one. Often the better
message names the rule instead: *expected expression*. A label does that, but only for a failure
that got no further than the rule's first character. A failure further in has expectations that say
more than the rule's name.

Replacing works in two steps. Before the rule runs, the label notes how many expectations are
already recorded at its start. If the rule fails and nothing got further, the ones the rule added
are dropped and the label's name takes their place.

        // The number of expectations recorded at offset `at`: -1 when nothing has failed that far yet.
        expectationsAt(at: int) -> int = if furthest < at then -1 else expectations.length()

        // Replaces what was recorded at offset `at` since `mark` (an `expectationsAt(at)` taken before)
        // by `what`: a failure that got no further than `at` is reported under one name.
        relabel(at: int, mark: int, what: string)
            if furthest != at then return
            val keep = if mark < 0 then 0 else mark
            while expectations.length() > keep
                expectations.remove(expectations.length() - 1)
            expectations.add(what)

### Lines and columns

A position is worked out only when it is asked for: for an error, once, and for `positioned`, once
per value. Counting from the start of the text each time would make a parse that asks for many
positions quadratic. The input keeps the last position it worked out and counts on from there when
the next one is further along, so positions asked for in order cost one pass over the text in all.

A column counts characters. In UTF-8 every byte of the form `10xxxxxx` continues a character, so a
character starts at every other byte, and only those advance the column.

        // The position of byte offset `at`, counted from the last position worked out when `at` lies
        // after it, so positions asked for in order cost one pass over the text.
        position(at: int) -> Position
            val from = if at >= known.offset then known else Position(0, 1, 1)
            var i = from.offset
            var line = from.line
            var column = from.column
            while i < at
                val b = int(text.byteAt(i))
                if b == 0x0a
                    line += 1
                    column = 1
                elif (b & 0xc0) != 0x80
                    column += 1
                i += 1
            known = Position(at, line, column)
            known

### The error

When the parse is over, the furthest failure becomes the error. Two alternatives can expect the same
thing at the same place, as `a ~ b | a ~ b ~ c` does, so the expectations are listed once each, in
the order they were first recorded. What was found is the character's `debug` form, quoted and
escaped, so a newline shows as `'\n'` rather than breaking the message across lines.

        // The error the parse reports: the furthest failure, with every distinct expectation there.
        error() -> ParseError
            val at = if furthest < 0 then offset else furthest
            val seen = Buffer[string]()
            for e in expectations
                if !seen.contains(e) then seen.add(e)
            val found = if at >= length then "end of input" else debug(text.charAt(at))
            ParseError(position(at), seen.toVector(), found)
